window.LOREFACT={"X_ASGARD_BURI_100":{"lore":"Buri is the first god of Norse myth, licked free from the salty primordial ice by the cosmic cow Audhumla over three days. He fathered Borr, whose sons Odin, Vili, and Ve shaped the world from the giant Ymir. He is the seed-ancestor of the whole Aesir line.","fact":"Buri is a deity from Snorri's Prose Edda with no historical existence whatsoever. He appears here only because medieval genealogists extended royal pedigrees back through Odin to a divine 'first being' for prestige. The birth year 100 AD and 'place' Asgard are game scaffolding, not history."},"X_ASGARD_BORR_135":{"lore":"Borr (Bur) is the son of Buri and the giantess Bestla's husband. With Bestla he fathered the three creator-gods Odin, Vili, and Ve. He is a bridge figure in the cosmogony, barely a character in his own right.","fact":"Borr is a mythological figure from the Eddas, not a real person; he has no documented existence. He sits in the pedigree purely as the genealogical link between the first god Buri and Odin. The dates and Asgard 'birthplace' are invented for the card."},"X_ASGARD_ODIN_165":{"lore":"Odin (Anglo-Saxon Woden) is the one-eyed All-Father who traded an eye at Mimir's well for wisdom and hung nine nights on Yggdrasil to win the runes. Lord of war, poetry, and the dead, he rides the eight-legged Sleipnir and is fated to fall to the wolf Fenrir at Ragnarok. Snorri euhemerized him as a migrating chieftain-king from whom Scandinavian and Anglo-Saxon royal houses descend.","fact":"Odin is the chief god of the Norse-Germanic pantheon and has no historical existence. Nearly every Yngling, Wessex, Mercian, and Northumbrian royal line was traced back to Woden by medieval genealogists to confer divine legitimacy. His placement at the head of multiple bloodlines is a deliberate medieval convention, not descent from a real man."},"X_ASGARD_NJORD_195":{"lore":"Njord is the Vanir god of the sea, wind, and wealth, who dwells at Noatun ('ship-haven'). Sent to the Aesir as a hostage after the Aesir-Vanir war, he married the giantess Skadi, but they could not agree whether to live by his shore or her mountains. He fathered Freyr and Freyja. Snorri recast him as an early earthly king of Sweden who brought peace and good harvests.","fact":"Njord is a Vanir deity, not a historical king; the 'King of Noatun' title comes from Snorri's euhemerizing of gods into Yngling ancestors. There is no documented person behind him. The auto-bio's claim of a 'Maxfield bloodline' mother and a birth in 195 AD is pure game fabrication."},"X_YNGLING_FREYR_225":{"lore":"Freyr (Yngvi-Freyr) is the Vanir god of fertility, sun, rain, and kingship, owner of the ship Skidbladnir and the golden boar Gullinbursti, who gave away his sword for love of the giantess Gerd. As Yngvi he is the divine founder of the Yngling dynasty; Snorri's Ynglinga saga makes him a king at Uppsala whose death was hidden three years while tribute was poured into his burial mound to keep the good seasons.","fact":"Freyr is a god, not a historical king, though the Ynglings genuinely traced their dynastic name (Ynglingar) to his byname Yngvi. The auto-bio's anchoring to the Gothic War (376-382) is a nonsense anachronism. The real kernel is only that a fertility-god cult at Uppsala underlies the dynasty's origin story."},"I350063587854":{"lore":"Fjolnir is the first mortal Yngling king, son of the god Freyr. In Ynglinga saga he drowns absurdly: visiting King Frodi in Denmark, he rises at night drunk, blunders along a gallery, falls through an opening into a huge vat of mead, and drowns in it. His ridiculous death is the archetype of the saga's pattern of strange royal ends.","fact":"Fjolnir is a legendary, not historical, figure known only from Ynglingatal (9th c.) and Snorri's 13th-c. Heimskringla, written centuries after the supposed events. No contemporary record exists. The death-place Hleithra (Lejre), Denmark, matches the saga's mead-vat tale, but the dates (256-312) are conventional back-calculations."},"I350063587855":{"lore":"In the sagas Fjolnir's wife is unnamed; this 'Vana' is a genealogical placeholder spouse rather than a figure with her own story. The name simply echoes the Vanir.","fact":"Not a distinct historical or even saga figure. She is a duplicate spouse-card built to give Fjolnir a wife, with the same death-place (Hleithra) copied from his drowning legend. No source names a wife of Fjolnir.","phantom":true},"X_WESSEX_BAELDAEG_260":{"lore":"Baeldaeg is the Anglo-Saxon reflex of the god Baldr, the beautiful, beloved son of Woden, in West Saxon royal genealogy. In Norse myth Baldr is killed by a mistletoe dart guided by Loki into the hand of the blind Hod, and his death is the first sign of Ragnarok. In the pedigrees he is simply Woden's son and the ancestor through whom Cerdic of Wessex descends.","fact":"Baeldaeg is a god (Baldr) inserted into the Wessex king-list, not a historical man. The West Saxon genealogies routinely placed a divine son of Woden at the head of the line. There is no record of any real person here; the dates and 'Asgard' birthplace are scaffolding."},"I350063587853":{"lore":"Snaer (Snow), called Snja the Old, is a frost-king of Kvenland in the legendary Fornaldarsaga material, descended from personified Frosti/Kari/Jokul (Frost, Wind, Glacier). His daughter Drifa ('snowdrift') marries into the Yngling line; another daughter Mjoll appears in related tales. He belongs to the mythic 'weather-giant' genealogy of Finland/Kvenland.","fact":"Snaer is a legendary personification of snow, not a historical king, found in late Icelandic genealogical fornaldarsogur. No documented person underlies him. The auto-bio's matter-of-fact treatment and the wandering death-place are artifacts; this is folklore, not record."},"I350063587852":{"lore":"Niera is given as Snaer's wife and Drifa's mother, but she is a genealogical filler-spouse with no saga episode of her own.","fact":"Not a distinct attested figure; a placeholder wife attached to the legendary snow-king Snaer. Her conflicting birth/death places (Raumsdal, then 'Heinsberg, Nordrhein-Westfalen') expose geocoding garbage rather than any real biography.","phantom":true},"I350063587850":{"lore":"Sveigdir (Sveigder) is the Yngling king who chased a dwarf into a stone. Told a great stone held the gods, he ran after a dwarf who stood in a boulder's doorway at evening; the dwarf called him in, the rock closed, and Sveigdir was never seen again. The vanishing-into-the-rock is his famous saga death.","fact":"Sveigdir is a legendary Yngling from Ynglingatal and Heimskringla, not documented history, written centuries after the fact. The stone-and-dwarf tale is pure folklore. The auto-bio omits it entirely; the dates are conventional. 'Skuttunge' is a real parish near Uppsala grafted onto his name."},"I350063587849":{"lore":"This 'Vana of Vanaheim' is the saga-wife of Sveigdir, said to be a woman of Vanaland (Vanaheim) he married on his eastern travels. In Ynglinga saga Sveigdir does marry a woman named Vana in 'Vanaland,' but she carries no independent story.","fact":"Vana is barely a saga figure and not historical; she exists only as Sveigdir's wife in a single line of Ynglinga saga. As a card she is essentially a spouse-placeholder echoing the Vanir name. No source gives her any life beyond the marriage.","phantom":true},"X_WESSEX_BRAND_285":{"lore":"Brand is a name in the West Saxon royal genealogy between Baeldaeg and the historical-claimed forebears of Cerdic. He has no myth or episode attached; he is one of several link-names padding the descent from Woden down to Cerdic.","fact":"Brand is a genealogical name-link in the Wessex pedigree with no recorded deeds and no independent attestation beyond the king-lists. Whether any real person stands behind the name is unknowable; the dates and English 'Wessex' birthplace are anachronistic scaffolding for a pre-Cerdic name."},"I350063587848":{"lore":"Vanlandi is the Yngling king who is trampled to death by a nightmare. Having abandoned his Finnish wife Drifa, he is cursed by the sorceress Huld; a 'mara' (mare/nightmare spirit) rides him in his sleep, crushing his legs then his head, and he dies. The word 'nightmare' itself echoes this tale.","fact":"Vanlandi is a legendary Yngling from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, not history. The mara/nightmare death is folklore. The auto-bio omits the story and the dramatic Finnish-curse motif. Dates are conventional reconstructions, not documented."},"I350063587847":{"lore":"Drifa ('snowdrift'), daughter of the snow-king Snaer, marries Vanlandi. When he deserts her, she hires the witch Huld and sends the deadly nightmare-spirit that kills him, then raises their son Visbur to avenge her. She is the wronged Finnish bride whose sorcery drives Vanlandi's doom.","fact":"Drifa is a legendary figure in Ynglinga saga, a personified-snow lineage daughter, not a historical woman. She has a genuine saga role (the curse on Vanlandi), unlike pure filler spouses, but no documented existence. Kvenland/Finland placement is from the legend, dates invented."},"I350063587846":{"lore":"This card represents Auda/Audr, the wife of Visbur and mother of Domaldi. In Ynglinga saga Visbur abandons his first wife (mother of two sons) for another; the spurned sons, denied their mother's bride-gold neck-ring, later burn their father alive in his hall. She is the mother whose grievance brings the curse of kin-burning on the Ynglings.","fact":"A legendary saga figure, not historical. The name here is garbled ('Queen Rich Audasdotter Authisdottir' stacks a mistranslated epithet onto a patronymic). The kernel is the saga's first-wife-of-Visbur; the auto-bio sanitizes it into a placid royal marriage, erasing the burning-curse story."},"X_FRANK_SUNNO_310":{"lore":"Sunno is one of the earliest named Frankish leaders, recorded (with Marcomer) by the Roman historian Sulpicius Alexander as a chieftain who raided across the Rhine into Roman Germania in the 380s-390s. In the legendary king-lists he was later strung into the ancestry of the Merovingians.","fact":"Sunno is semi-historical: a real Frankish war-leader of the late 4th century is attested in Roman sources, but his role as a dynastic 'King of the Ripuarian Franks' fathering Marcomer is a later genealogical construction. The neat 310 birth and tidy succession are legendary embroidery on a thin documentary kernel."},"X_WESSEX_FRITHEGAR_310":{"lore":"Frithegar (Frithugar) is a link-name in the West Saxon genealogy between Brand and Freawine, with no surviving story or deeds. He simply fills the descent from Woden toward Cerdic.","fact":"Frithegar is a bare genealogical name in the Wessex king-lists, unattested outside them and with no recorded actions. No real person can be confirmed behind the name; dates and Wessex birthplace are scaffolding for a figure who predates any English settlement of Wessex."},"I350063587845":{"lore":"Visbur is the Yngling king burned alive in his own hall by his sons. Having cast off their mother Auda and withheld her gold neck-ring, he is targeted by his vengeful sons, who, advised by the witch Huld, set fire to his hall and burn him to death. His burning begins a recurring curse of kin-slaying in the line.","fact":"Visbur is a legendary Yngling from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, not documented history. The hall-burning by his sons is his defining saga episode, which the auto-bio omits entirely in favor of a generic 'navigated alliances' summary. Dates are conventional, not recorded."},"X_WESSEX_FREAWINE_335":{"lore":"Freawine appears in the Wessex genealogy and in Saxo Grammaticus as a legendary West Saxon hero-ealdorman who fought the Swedes; in some tales he is slain in single combat by the Swedish king and avenged by his son Wig. He is one of the more storied link-names in the line.","fact":"Freawine is a legendary figure of the Wessex pedigree and Scandinavian saga (Saxo), not a documented historical king. Any kernel is heroic folklore, not record. His place in Cerdic's ancestry is a genealogical construction; the 335 date and English Wessex setting are anachronistic."},"I350063587844":{"lore":"Domaldi is the Yngling king sacrificed by his own people to end a famine. Through three autumns of failed harvests the Swedes offered first oxen, then men, with no relief; in the third year the chieftains agreed the king himself was the price, slew Domaldi, and reddened the altars with his blood for better seasons. He is the great human-sacrifice king of the saga.","fact":"Domaldi is legendary, from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, not history. His sacrifice for harvest is a famous example of sacral kingship in the sagas but is not documented fact. The auto-bio absurdly references the Battle of Brunanburh (937), centuries too late and unrelated. Dates are invented."},"X_MEROV_MARCOMER_340":{"lore":"Marcomer is a Frankish chieftain recorded by Roman writers leading raids across the Rhine into Roman Gaul in the 380s-390s; he was eventually seized by the Romans and exiled. Later Merovingian legend made him father of Pharamond and an ancestor of the dynasty.","fact":"Marcomer is semi-historical: a genuine late-4th-century Frankish leader attested in Roman sources, but his dynastic role as a 'King of the Franks' fathering the legendary Pharamond is a later medieval graft. The Attila reference in the auto-bio is anachronistic (Attila rose decades after). Real kernel, legendary frame."},"X_BURGUNDY_GIBICA_350":{"lore":"Gibica (Gjuki in Norse, Gibich in German) is the founding king of the Burgundian royal house, named in the Lex Burgundionum as an ancestral king. In the heroic legend of the Nibelungs he becomes Gjuki/Gibich, father of Gunnar/Gunther and Gudrun/Kriemhild, at whose court the Burgundian tragedy unfolds.","fact":"Gibica is quasi-historical: the Burgundian law-code names him as a real ancestral king (early 5th c.), and he is the historical seed of the legendary Gjuki/Gibich of the Nibelung cycle. The precise 350 birth and tidy succession to Gondicar are genealogical smoothing of a sparsely documented figure."},"X_WESSEX_WIG_360":{"lore":"Wig is a West Saxon ancestor-name in Cerdic's genealogy, son of Freawine; in the Freawine legend it is Wig who avenges his father by killing the Swedish king. Otherwise he is a link in the descent from Woden to Gewis and Cerdic.","fact":"Wig is a legendary/genealogical name in the Wessex king-lists, attested only there and in the associated heroic tale. He is not a documented historical king; his role in Cerdic's pedigree is a construction. The 360 date and Wessex birthplace are anachronistic scaffolding."},"I350063587843":{"lore":"Domar is the rare Yngling who dies peacefully of sickness in his bed at Uppsala and is burned on the bank of the Fyris river. His long, uneventful reign is a deliberate calm between his father Domaldi's sacrifice and his son Dyggvi's death, and Ynglingatal notes simply that 'sickness took' him.","fact":"Domar is a legendary Yngling from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, not documented history. The peaceful-death detail is from the saga. His wife Drott, daughter of Danp, ties the line into the legendary Danish/Dan ancestry. Dates (361-437) are conventional reconstructions."},"I350063587842":{"lore":"Drott, daughter of Danp son of Rig, is Domar's wife and mother of Dyggvi, the marriage that (per Snorri) first brought the royal title 'konungr' into the Yngling line through her Danish kin. She is a genealogical bridge to the Rig-Danp royal-name legend rather than a figure with episodes.","fact":"Drott is a minor legendary figure in Ynglinga saga whose only significance is dynastic (linking Ynglings to the Danp/Rig naming legend). Not historical. The auto-bio's death-place 'Svearne, Norway' is geocoding confusion; dates are invented."},"X_MEROV_PHARAMOND_370":{"lore":"Pharamond (Faramund) is the legendary first king of the Franks, named in the 7th-century Liber Historiae Francorum as son of Marcomer and the law-giver from whom later Salic-law tradition descends. Medieval and early-modern romance made him a founding hero-king, though he appears in no contemporary record.","fact":"Pharamond is legendary, not historical: he first appears two centuries after his supposed lifetime and is absent from all 5th-century sources. He functions as a dynastic origin-figure for the Merovingians. The Rhine-crossing of 406 in the auto-bio is real history but only loosely pinned to him for color."},"X_BURGUNDY_GONDICAR_380":{"lore":"Gondicar (Gundahar/Gunther) is the historical Burgundian king at Worms whose kingdom was annihilated by Hunnic federates around 436-437 - the catastrophe that became the legend of the Nibelungs' fall at Etzel's (Attila's) court. He is the real king behind the doomed Gunther/Gunnar of the heroic epics.","fact":"Gondicar/Gundahar is genuinely historical: a Burgundian king attested in Roman sources, killed with much of his people by Huns c.437, and the kernel of the Nibelungenlied's Gunther. The genealogy's neat father (Gibica) and son (Gundioc) compress real but tangled Burgundian succession; the 380 date is approximate."},"I350063587839":{"lore":"Dyggvi is a Yngling king claimed in death by Hel herself: Ynglingatal says the goddess of the dead, daughter of Loki, 'chose' and embraced Dyggvi, taking the king as her lover-corpse. He is notable as the first Yngling said to have died of sickness as a true 'king' (konungr) by his mother's Danish descent.","fact":"Dyggvi is a legendary Yngling from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, not history. The Hel-claims-him verse is the famous detail, omitted by the auto-bio. The card's listed 'spouse' is corrupt (it repeats Dyggvi's own name). Dates (382-481, a 99-year life) are impossible conventional padding."},"X_WESSEX_GEWIS_385":{"lore":"Gewis is the eponymous ancestor of the Gewisse, the early name of the West Saxon people, in Cerdic's genealogy. He is a back-formed founder-figure invented to explain the tribal name 'Gewisse' rather than a hero with his own deeds.","fact":"Gewis is a legendary eponym, not a historical person - a name coined to account for the tribal label Gewisse. Such eponymous ancestors are a standard feature of Anglo-Saxon genealogies. The 385 date and Wessex birthplace are anachronistic scaffolding for a pre-migration name."},"I350063587840":{"lore":"This card is a duplicate/corrupt re-listing of Dyggvi as his own spouse; it carries no distinct legend. The death-place 'Y, Somme' is a real French commune name produced by geocoding, not a saga event.","fact":"Not a real distinct figure. It is a malformed spouse-card mirroring Dyggvi (same name, paired as his wife), with a geocoded death at 'Y, Somme' in France that has nothing to do with the Uppsala kings. No saga gives Dyggvi a named wife of this form.","phantom":true},"X_MEROV_CLODION_395":{"lore":"Clodion the Long-Haired is the early Frankish king (fl. c.430-450) who pushed the Salian Franks from Toxandria into Roman Gaul as far as the Somme, taking Tournai and Cambrai before being checked by the Roman general Aetius. Gregory of Tours names him, and later tradition made him father (or kin) of Merovech.","fact":"Clodion is largely historical: a real Salian Frankish king attested by Gregory of Tours, though his exact link to Merovech and Pharamond is legendary. He is one of the firmest figures in this otherwise mythical stretch. The 'McCollum bloodline mother' in the auto-bio is pure game fabrication."},"X_FINLAND_FROSTI_400":{"lore":"Frosti ('Frost') is a personified weather-king of Finland/Kvenland in the legendary snow-genealogy (Frosti, son of Snaer's line, Kari=wind, Jokul=glacier). His daughter Skjalf marries the Yngling king Agni - and avenges her father by strangling Agni with his own gold neck-ring.","fact":"Frosti is a legendary personification of frost from the Icelandic fornaldarsogur, not a historical king of Finland. No documented person underlies him. He exists in the pedigree only to father Skjalf and motivate her revenge on Agni. Dates are invented scaffolding."},"I350063587838":{"lore":"Dag the Wise is the Yngling king who understood the speech of birds. He kept a sacred sparrow that flew far gathering news; when a farmer in Gotland (Vorvi/Reidgotaland) killed the bird, Dag marched to avenge it and was struck dead by a thrown hayfork (or pitch-fork) in the battle at Vorvi. His war over a sparrow is his defining tale.","fact":"Dag is a legendary Yngling from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, not history. The talking-sparrow vengeance and death by a farmhand's fork at Vorvi are the famous details, absent from the auto-bio. The listed 'spouse' is corrupt (repeats his own name); the 90-year lifespan and dates are conventional padding."},"I350063587837":{"lore":"Dag the Wise was a Yngling king of Uppsala so wise he understood the speech of birds, kept a tame sparrow that flew far gathering news for him. When men in Reidgotaland (Gotland/Vendsyssel) killed his sparrow, Dag invaded to avenge it and was struck dead by a thrown hayfork (or pitchfork) at a ford. He is a son of Dyggvi in Ynglingatal.","fact":"Dag is a legendary Yngling king known only from Snorri's Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, poetry set down centuries after the supposed events; he is not a documented historical person. The card is also mis-sexed and geo-garbled: 'Died in Y, Somme' is a geocoding artifact (an Uppsala king did not die in Picardy), and the 'spouse' field just re-states his own name. The dates (407-429) are invented genealogical fill."},"X_WESSEX_ESLA_410":{"lore":"Esla appears only as a name in the West Saxon royal pedigree of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle and Asser: Cerdic son of Elesa son of Esla son of Gewis son of Wig, the line traced back to Woden. He has no story of his own beyond being a link in the chain to the god Woden.","fact":"Esla is a bare name in the legendary Wessex genealogy with no recorded deeds, no death, and no independent attestation. Such early names are widely regarded as artificial back-formations inserted to give Cerdic a Woden-descended pedigree, not real historical individuals. The 410 birth year is a genealogist's estimate."},"X_MEROV_MEROVECH_411":{"lore":"Merovech (Merovius) is the legendary namesake of the Merovingian dynasty, said to be son or kinsman of Chlodio and father of Childeric I. Legend claims he was sired by a sea-beast (the 'Quinotaur') that beset his mother, giving the dynasty a supernatural origin; he was reputed to have fought at the Catalaunian Plains against Attila in 451.","fact":"Merovech is quasi-legendary: the dynasty bears his name but he is barely documented, known mainly from Gregory of Tours who himself was unsure he existed. The sea-monster parentage is myth. A real Frankish kindred underlies the Merovingians, but Merovech as a person sits on the boundary of history and legend."},"I350063587836":{"lore":"Agni was a Yngling king who raided Finland and killed its king Frosti, carrying off the daughter Skjalf and a great gold neck-ring (the Visburs-torque). At the wedding feast at Agnafit (Stockholm) he drank himself to sleep; Skjalf, avenging her father, tied a cord to his gold ring while he slept and her men hauled him up by it, hanging him from a tree. The place Agnafit is named for him.","fact":"Agni is a legendary Yngling king from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, attested only in poetry recorded centuries later; not a historical figure. The card's bio omits the famous hanging entirely and mis-labels his murderer-wife as merely a spouse; 'Agnefit, Sweden' is the one genuine saga detail. Dates are genealogical invention."},"I350063587835":{"lore":"Skjalf (Skjalf), daughter of the Finnish king Frosti, was seized by Agni after he slew her father. In revenge she got Agni drunk at the Agnafit feast, fastened a rope to the heavy gold ring on his neck while he slept, and had her followers hoist him up and hang him from a tree \u2014 then escaped. She is one of the saga's great avenging women, not a contented queen.","fact":"Skjalf is a legendary figure from the Yngling tradition, known only through Snorri's much-later prose; no historical existence. The current bio inverts her story into a placid royal marriage and adds a false 'Maxfield bloodline' gloss; 'Finland'/'Svitjod' reflect the saga, the precise dates do not."},"X_BURGUNDY_GUNDIOC_430":{"lore":"Gundioc (Gundioch) was a king of the Burgundians who settled his people in Sapaudia (Savoy) and the Rh\u00f4ne valley under late-Roman authority, allied with the magister militum Ricimer (his kinsman by marriage). He divided the kingdom among his sons, including Chilperic and Gundobad, founding the line that produced Saint Clotilde.","fact":"Gundioc is the most genuinely historical figure on this card: a real 5th-century Burgundian king attested in contemporary sources like Sidonius Apollinaris and Gregory of Tours. The card's frame ('legendary', dynastic Maxfield-style framing) understates him; his deeds above are solidly documented, though exact dates remain approximate."},"X_WESSEX_ELESA_435":{"lore":"Elesa is named in the West Saxon pedigree as son of Esla and father of Cerdic, first king of Wessex \u2014 one more rung in the chain the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle traces from Cerdic back through Gewis to the god Woden. He carries no narrative of his own.","fact":"Elesa is a name-only ancestor in the legendary Wessex genealogy with no recorded acts and no independent evidence he existed; like Esla he is generally judged an artificial pedigree-link to Woden. The 435 date is an estimate built to space the generations."},"X_MEROV_CHILDERIC_I_437":{"lore":"Childeric I was king of the Salian Franks at Tournai and father of Clovis I. He served as a Roman ally in Gaul, fighting alongside Aegidius against the Visigoths and Saxons, and married Basina of Thuringia. His richly furnished grave (gold bees, signet ring reading CHILDERICI REGIS) was unearthed at Tournai in 1653.","fact":"Childeric I is genuinely historical \u2014 attested by Gregory of Tours and confirmed by his 1653-discovered tomb \u2014 though wrapped in legendary embroidery. The card mis-files him as purely 'legendary' and repeats a garbled chronology (the 'Visigoths sacked Rome 450/476' line is muddled). His real reign is roughly 458-481."},"I350063587833":{"lore":"Alrek and his brother Eirik were joint Yngling kings, famed horsemen who loved to ride. One day, riding out alone, the two brothers quarreled and beat each other to death with their horse-bridles, having no other weapons to hand \u2014 a death Ynglingatal singles out as strange.","fact":"Alrek is a legendary Yngling king from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla; not historical. The card omits the bridle-death story, gives an absurd 91-year lifespan (445-536), and adds the spurious 'Maxfield bloodline'. Places like Noatun are mythic (Njord's hall), not real biography."},"I350063587832":{"lore":"In the saga Alrek's queen is Dageid, daughter of King Dag the Mighty (Dagr) of the Dagling line; she is the mother of Alf and Yngvi. She has no episode of her own \u2014 her role is purely genealogical, marrying the Yngling line to the Daglings.","fact":"Dageid is a legendary genealogical figure attached to the Yngling pedigree, known only from late saga prose; no historical existence. The inflated name 'Dagreid Dageith Sweden Dagsdotter' and the 'Maxfield bloodline' are database artifacts, and the precise 449-481 dates are invented."},"X_BURGUNDY_CHILPERIC_II_450":{"lore":"Chilperic II was a Burgundian king, son of Gundioc, who ruled a share of the kingdom from Geneva or Lyon alongside his brothers. In the dynastic bloodletting he was killed by his brother Gundobad (his wife reputedly drowned with a stone round her neck); his daughter Clotilde survived to marry Clovis I and bring Catholic Christianity to the Franks.","fact":"Chilperic II is broadly historical, attested by Gregory of Tours as one of Gundioc's sons and father of the well-documented Queen Clotilde, though details of his death are semi-legendary. The 'legendary' tag undersells him; dates are approximate."},"I350063587829":{"lore":"Yngvi and his brother Alf were sons of Alrek. Yngvi was a great warrior, away raiding each summer; the homebody Alf grew jealous of how Yngvi sat late talking with Alf's wife Bera. One night Alf stabbed Yngvi, and the dying Yngvi ran his own sword through Alf \u2014 the brothers killed each other in the hall.","fact":"Yngvi is a legendary Yngling king from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla; not historical. This card is badly mangled \u2014 the 'spouse' field repeats his own name, 'Died in Alf, Cochem-Zell' geocodes his killer-brother Alf into a German district, and the reign dates are fabricated."},"X_MEROV_CLOVIS_I_466":{"lore":"Clovis I united the Frankish tribes, defeated Syagrius at Soissons (486) ending the last Roman rule in Gaul, beat the Alemanni at Tolbiac, and \u2014 urged by his wife Clotilde \u2014 converted to Catholic (not Arian) Christianity, baptized at Reims. He crushed the Visigoths at Vouill\u00e9 (507) and founded the Merovingian kingdom.","fact":"Clovis I is fully historical, one of the best-attested figures of the 5th-6th centuries (Gregory of Tours and contemporary letters). Placing him in a 'legendary' tier is wrong; the card's chronology is loose (he reigned 481-511) but the man and deeds are documented fact."},"X_WESSEX_CERDIC_467":{"lore":"Cerdic is named in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle as the founder and first king of Wessex, said to have landed in Hampshire with his son Cynric around 495, fought the Britons, and taken the Isle of Wight before dying in 534. The royal house of Wessex (down to Alfred) traced itself to him.","fact":"Cerdic is legendary/semi-legendary; the Chronicle was compiled ~400 years later and his very name appears to be Brittonic (Carat\u012bcos), raising doubt he was the Saxon founder later tradition made him. There may be a genuine kernel of a Wessex founding figure, but his deeds and dates are not reliable history."},"I350063587830":{"lore":"This entry corresponds to Yngvi of the Ynglings (the warrior brother slain alongside Alf). There is no separate 'wife Yngvi' in the saga \u2014 the figure is simply Yngvi himself, doubled.","fact":"Not a distinct person: this is a duplicate of the Yngling king Yngvi Alreksson, generated as his own 'spouse' (the spouse field literally re-lists his name). Like the original it is legendary, not historical, and carries the same geocoding garbage ('Died in Y, Somme').","phantom":true},"I350063587825":{"lore":"Jorund (Jorundr) and his brother Eirik were sea-king Ynglings who raided widely. Jorund killed King Gudlaug of the Halogalanders by hanging. Later, in Jutland at the Limfjord (Oddesund), Jorund was defeated by Gylaug, Gudlaug's son, captured, and hanged in revenge \u2014 death by the rope, as he had dealt it.","fact":"Jorund is a legendary Yngling king from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla; not historical. The card garbles his parentage (it names his own wife Frodi as his 'mother') and the dates are invented, but 'Addasund, Limfjord' does echo the real saga hanging-site (Oddesund in the Limfjord)."},"I350063587824":{"lore":"In Norse legend 'Fr\u00f3\u00f0i' is a famous Danish peace-king of Hleidra (Lejre), in whose reign the magic mill Grotti ground out gold and peace \u2014 the male Skjoldung king Frodi, not a Swedish queen. As a wife-of-Jorund this is a genealogical graft onto the Danish royal name.","fact":"Legendary: Fr\u00f3\u00f0i belongs to the Danish Skjoldung legend (the Grotti-mill peace-king), with no historical existence, and Hleithra/Lejre is the legendary Danish royal seat. Here a male Danish king's name has been turned into 'Frodi Fridleifsdottir, Queen of Sweden' and married into the Ynglings \u2014 a fabricated, sex-changed pedigree link.","phantom":true},"X_WESSEX_CYNRIC_495":{"lore":"Cynric, son (or grandson) of Cerdic, is the second king of Wessex in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle. He is credited with victories over the Britons at Searobyrig (Old Sarum, 552) and Beranburh (556) with his son Ceawlin, extending Saxon power in the upper Thames.","fact":"Cynric is legendary/semi-legendary, from the much-later Chronicle; the battle entries are traditional, not contemporary record. A real early Wessex dynasty may stand behind the names, but his individual dates and deeds are not securely historical."},"X_MEROV_CHLOTHAR_I_497":{"lore":"Chlothar I (Clotaire), youngest son of Clovis I, shared the Frankish realm with his brothers and outlived them all to reunite the whole kingdom by 558. He absorbed Burgundy, campaigned in Thuringia and Saxony, and saw his last years troubled by the revolt of his son Chramn, whom he had burned to death with his family.","fact":"Chlothar I is fully historical, well attested by Gregory of Tours; reigned 511-561. The 'legendary' tier and the lone 'had Blithildis 540' event are reductive \u2014 Blithildis herself is a disputed later-genealogical figure \u2014 but Chlothar the man is documented."},"I350063587823":{"lore":"Aun (\u00c1ni) the Old was a Yngling king of Uppsala who sacrificed his sons to Odin one by one to buy longer life \u2014 nine sons in all. With each sacrifice Odin granted him ten more years, until he grew so feeble he had to be fed from a horn like an infant; the people stopped him before he could sacrifice his tenth and last son, and he died. '\u00c1nas\u00f3tt' (Aun's-sickness) became a byword for senile decline.","fact":"Aun is a legendary Yngling king from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla; not historical. The card entirely omits the famous nine-sons sacrifice and gives him an ordinary 35-year life (509-544) with a normal heir, stripping the one thing he is known for. 'The Dengamie'/'av Svitjod' are garbled saga epithets."},"I350063587822":{"lore":"Aun's queen is unnamed in the main Yngling tradition; 'Hervor Helgesdatter' is a genealogist's graft. The vivid saga focus is entirely on Aun and his sacrificed sons, not on a named wife \u2014 she carries no episode of her own.","fact":"Legendary and largely fabricated: a name supplied to give Aun a wife in a database pedigree, with no saga story and no historical existence. The dates (513-535) are invented and 'Died in Y, Somme' is geocoding garbage placing an Uppsala queen in Picardy."},"I350063587820":{"lore":"Egil \u2014 also Ongentheow in the Old English tradition (Beowulf) and 'Vendilcrow' in Norse \u2014 was a Yngling/Scylfing king. In Ynglingatal a thrall or a bull (the 'Tunni'/bull) he had wronged gored or hunted him to death; in Beowulf Ongentheow is a fierce old Swedish king killed by the Geats at the battle of Ravenswood. The two traditions remember the same Uppsala king from opposite shores.","fact":"Quasi-legendary: known from Ynglingatal and, as Ongentheow, from Beowulf \u2014 the Vendel-era Uppsala kings (Egil/Ottar/Adils) are the layer of this list with a possible genuine kernel, echoed in the rich Vendel/Old Uppsala mound burials. Still, no documented biography; dates and the 'Maxfield bloodline' are invention."},"I350063587821":{"lore":"There is no queen 'Onelan Modor' in the Norse or English sources. The name is an artifact: in Beowulf the Swedish royal women's line is barely mentioned, and 'modor' is simply Old English for 'mother'. A common noun (and a garbled male name, Onela) has been mistaken for a person.","fact":"Not a real figure. 'Modor' is the Old English word 'mother' misread as a surname, and 'Onela' is actually the name of a male Scylfing king (Ongentheow's grandson) \u2014 the two have been fused into a phantom wife for Egil. No historical or even coherent legendary existence; 'Died in Y, Somme' is geocoding garbage.","phantom":true},"X_WESSEX_CEAWLIN_545":{"lore":"Ceawlin, son of Cynric, was one of the great early kings of Wessex, listed among the Bretwaldas (over-kings of Britain). The Chronicle credits him with the victory at Dyrham (577) that took Gloucester, Cirencester and Bath from the Britons and split the western Welsh from those of the south-west; he was driven out and died in 593.","fact":"Ceawlin is semi-legendary but closer to history than his forebears \u2014 the Tribal/Bretwalda tradition and the Dyrham campaign may preserve real 6th-century events, though recorded centuries later in the Chronicle. His individual dates remain approximate, not contemporary record."},"I350063587818":{"lore":"Ottar (\u00d3ttarr) Vendilcrow, son of Egil, warred with the Danes. He raided Denmark while the Danish kings V\u00f6tt and Fasti were away; they retaliated, caught and killed Ottar in Vendel (Jutland) or at the Limfjord, then left his body on a mound for beasts and birds \u2014 calling him 'Vendel-crow' in mockery, the epithet that stuck.","fact":"Quasi-legendary Vendel-era Scylfing king from Ynglingatal, also reflected in Beowulf (Ohthere). Like Egil and Adils he belongs to the layer with a plausible historical kernel tied to the Vendel/Old Uppsala archaeology, but no documented life; the precise 551-576 dates are genealogical fill."},"X_WESSEX_CUTHWINE_570":{"lore":"Cuthwine appears in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle genealogy as a son of Ceawlin and ancestor of the later Wessex kings through his sons Cutha, Cynebald and Cedda; unlike his father he never reigned, the kingship having passed to a collateral line. He is a pedigree-bridge, not a king with a battle-story.","fact":"Cuthwine is a legendary/genealogical figure from the West Saxon king-list, attested only in the later Chronicle pedigree with no recorded deeds. The line back through him to Alfred is the traditional Wessex descent; his dates are estimates."},"I350063587813":{"lore":"Adils (Eadgils), son of Ottar, is the famous Uppsala king of the Beowulf/Norse cycle. He warred with his uncle Ali (Onela) and won the battle on the ice of Lake V\u00e4nern with Geatish help (and the hero B\u00f6dvar Bjarki in the Hrolf Kraki saga). Greedy for treasure, he died when his horse stumbled and he was thrown, dashing his head on a stone, during the disar-sacrifice at Uppsala.","fact":"Quasi-legendary Vendel-era king (Eadgils in Beowulf) \u2014 the Adils/Ottar/Egil group is the part of this list most likely to reflect real 6th-century Uppsala rulers, consonant with the great mounds at Gamla Uppsala. Still no documented biography; the 22-year lifespan and exact dates are invented, though 'Kings Mound, Old (Uppsala)' echoes the real burial tradition."},"I350063587814":{"lore":"Yrsa is the tragic queen of the Hrolf Kraki cycle: daughter of Helgi by Olof, she was unknowingly married to her own father Helgi (bearing Hrolf Kraki), then taken by the Swedish king Adils. She helped Hrolf escape Uppsala with Adils's gold, scattering it on the Fyris plains to delay pursuit. Her life is one of incest, abduction and divided loyalty.","fact":"Yrsa is a legendary figure from the Skjoldung/Hrolf Kraki sagas, not historical. The card flattens her dramatic story and adds pure geocoding garbage: 'Died in Ad, Maluku' places her in the Indonesian Moluccas, and the bio's claim of 'family ties to Indonesia' is the artifact restated as fact."},"I350063587810":{"lore":"In the Yngling line Ingvar's queen / Anund's mother is a Gautish woman of the royal house of Gautland (the 'daughter of a Gaut'), a marriage tying the Ynglings to the kings of the Geats. She has no episode of her own; her role is the Gautland alliance.","fact":"Legendary genealogical figure with no saga story and no historical existence. The label 'Gautrek Gautdottir' garbles a descriptor ('a Gaut's daughter') into a name borrowed from the male legendary king Gautrek; 'Died in Somme, Picardie' is geocoding garbage. Dates invented.","phantom":true},"I350063587812":{"lore":"Eystein, son of Adils, was a Yngling king of Uppsala. While he feasted, the sea-king S\u00f6lvi (Solve) of Jutland surrounded his hall by night, set it ablaze, and burned Eystein to death inside \u2014 death by hall-fire, a recurring fate of these kings.","fact":"Quasi-legendary Vendel-era Yngling king from Ynglingatal; not documented history. The card omits the burning-in-the-hall, garbles his wife into 'Queen Eyenstein Adillson' (a near-copy of his own name), and 'Died in Lofond, Maelar Lake' loosely echoes the saga's Lake M\u00e4laren setting. Dates fabricated."},"I350063587811":{"lore":"Eystein's queen is unnamed or only faintly named in the Yngling tradition; here she is a placeholder spouse for Eystein, her 'name' merely a feminized echo of his. She carries no legend of her own.","fact":"Not a distinct figure: 'Queen Eyenstein Adillson' is a duplicate-spouse artifact built from King Eystein's own name and patronymic. No saga story, no historical existence; the 'three sons' bio and 599-616 dates are database invention.","phantom":true},"X_SWEDEN_ANUND_610":{"lore":"Anund (Braut-\u00d6nund, 'Road-Anund'), son of Ingvar, was a Yngling king remembered as a great clearer of forests and builder of roads and settlements across Sweden \u2014 a peace-and-prosperity king rather than a warrior. He died when a landslide (or rockfall) at Himinheid buried him, crushed under stones in a ravine.","fact":"Anund is a legendary Yngling king from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla; not historical. The card conflates his name with his son's ('Anund Braut-Ingjald'), omits the road-building and the rockslide death, and dates the Viking Age to '793, eighty years after his birth' \u2014 an anachronistic muddle. Dates invented."},"X_GAUTLAND_GAUTREK_610":{"lore":"Gautrek the Generous is the hero-king of Gautland in Gautreks saga, famed for reckless open-handedness \u2014 he once gave away ship, treasure and even his own oath on a whim to the foolish Ref and to passing beggars. The saga frames him as the over-generous foil whose court attracts gift-seekers from across the North.","fact":"Gautrek is a purely legendary saga-king (Gautreks saga, a late fornaldarsaga), not historical. The card's bio is wildly anachronistic \u2014 it has him living through 'Harald Bluetooth around 950 AD' while born in 610 \u2014 a clear sign of auto-generated filler. Birth on 'Gotland' confuses Gotland the island with G\u00f6taland the mainland."},"I350063587809":{"lore":"Ingvar (the Tall), son of Eystein, was a Yngling king who fought the raiding Estonians (the 'men of Estland'/Sysla across the Baltic). Campaigning in the eastern lands, he fell in battle on the Estonian shore and was buried there in a mound by the sea, where the waves and 'the song of the East-sea' wash his grave in Ynglingatal.","fact":"Ingvar is a legendary Yngling king from Ynglingatal/Heimskringla; not historical, though the Baltic-raiding motif fits the genuine Vendel-age Swedish reach across the sea. 'Killed in battle in Stein, Estonia' echoes the saga's Estonian death-place; the exact 616-642 dates and 'Maxfield bloodline' are invention."},"I350063587802":{"lore":"In Ynglingatal/Heimskringla, Anund (Braut-Onund, 'Road-builder') was a prosperous, peace-loving Swedish king who cleared forests and built roads across Sweden's wilderness, settling the land. His byname 'Land-Clearer' comes from this road- and farm-building. Snorri says he was finally killed by a rockslide/landslide at a place called Himinheidi while traveling.","fact":"A legendary Yngling king of Uppsala known only from the late skaldic poem Ynglingatal and Snorri's 13th-century Heimskringla, not from contemporary record. There is no documented historical Anund; the dates here are genealogists' guesses. The death 'in an avalanche near Himenheith' echoes the real saga motif, but the precise year and age are invented."},"I350063587801":{"lore":"This card re-lists Anund Braut's supposed wife. In the actual sagas Anund's queen is not individually named in any memorable episode; 'Algaut' is in fact the name of a male king of Gautland elsewhere in this very pedigree, not a wife. The 'died in Iceland' detail is impossible: Iceland was uninhabited until c.870, two centuries after this date.","fact":"Not a genuine distinct historical person. The card appears to be a garbled spouse-slot built around the male name Algaut (King of Gautland), with an anachronistic Iceland death. No saga or record supports a woman of this name married to Anund.","phantom":true},"X_SWEDEN_INGJALD_640":{"lore":"Ingjald Ill-Ruler (Ingjald illradi) is the saga's most infamous Yngling: as a boy he ate a wolf's heart to gain ferocity, then as king invited the neighboring petty-kings to a feast at Uppsala and burned them alive in the hall to seize their lands. Cornered at last by Ivar Vidfamne, he and his daughter Asa chose to burn themselves and their household rather than be taken.","fact":"A legendary king of Sweden's Yngling line, known only from Ynglingatal and Heimskringla, with no contemporary documentation. The 'last Yngling king of Sweden' role is a saga literary device. This card duplicates the better-formed Ingjald 'The Wicked' card elsewhere in the shard; the dynastic detail (father Anund) is correct to the saga."},"X_GAUTLAND_ALGAUT_640":{"lore":"Algaut, king of Gautland (Gotaland, the mainland Gothic region, NOT the Baltic island Gotland), appears in Heimskringla as the father of Gauthild, whom he married to the Yngling king Onund/Anund. He is a minor frame-character whose role is to supply a royal bride and a Gautish alliance to the Uppsala dynasty.","fact":"A legendary figure from Heimskringla with no historical documentation; he exists chiefly to anchor a marriage alliance in the saga genealogy. The birthplace 'Gotland island' is a geocoding error for mainland Gotaland/Gautland in southern Sweden."},"I350063587789":{"lore":"Sveide 'the Sea-King' belongs to the Norwegian petty-king genealogies feeding the Vestfold/Ynglinga line. Sea-kings (saekonungar) in the sagas were leaders who 'never slept under a sooty roof-beam' and lived from their longships, raiding the coasts. He is a connective ancestor figure rather than the subject of a single famous episode.","fact":"A legendary/quasi-legendary name in the Norwegian royal pedigrees, attested only in late genealogical saga material, not contemporary record. The note that he 'had Halfdan' in 745 while dying in 699 is an internal chronological impossibility typical of these patched-together lineages."},"X_DALRIADA_FERFUITH_650":{"lore":"A connective ancestor in the Dal Riata (Scottish-Gaelic) king-list, presented as father of Selbach and forebear of the line running down to Kenneth MacAlpin. He carries no saga episode of his own; he is a genealogical link toward the later Scottish royal house.","fact":"The Dal Riata kings of this era (e.g. Selbach mac Ferchair, d.730) are partly historical, recorded in the Irish annals, though early generations blur into legend. 'Ferfuith' looks like a corrupted rendering of a Gaelic patronymic (cf. Ferchar); the specific person/dates here are reconstructed, not documented."},"I350063587788":{"lore":"This card re-lists Sveide the Sea-King's wife, variously called Hild/Hildur/Alfhild of Vestfold. She has no independent saga episode; her function is to be the queen who carries the Vestfold inheritance into the line. The bio's claim that her son Halfdan 'became King of Sweden' is an error \u2014 the Vestfold Halfdans were Norwegian.","fact":"Not an independently attested historical person; a spouse-slot in the Norwegian petty-king genealogy with no contemporary documentation and an internally contradictory chronology (child 'Halfdan' dated 745, 45+ years after her stated 700 death).","phantom":true},"I350063587800":{"lore":"This is the canonical Ingjald Ill-Ruler ('the Wicked'), last Yngling king of Sweden: he ate a wolf-heart for cruelty as a boy, then burned six (or more) rival kings alive in his great hall at Uppsala to swallow their realms, and at the end burned himself and his daughter Asa to death rather than surrender to Ivar Vidfamne. He married Gauthild of Gautland.","fact":"Legendary king known only from Ynglingatal and Heimskringla, undocumented in any contemporary source. The death 'in Raening Hall, Kanem' is a geocoding artifact \u2014 Kanem is in Chad; the saga's Raening is in Sweden. The 'last Yngling king of Sweden' framing is saga tradition, not history."},"I350063587799":{"lore":"Gauthild was the daughter of King Algaut of Gautland, given in marriage to Ingjald Ill-Ruler to bind Gautland to Uppsala. In Heimskringla she is the mother of Olaf Tretelgja ('Tree-Feller'). Her famous moment is her warning instinct: she sent her son Olaf away to safety in Nerike, which spared him from her husband Ingjald's self-immolation.","fact":"A legendary figure from Heimskringla, undocumented historically; she functions as the marriage-alliance bride and mother of the next link in the line. The dates (born 664, died 682 'after eight years') are genealogists' inventions."},"X_NORWAY_OLAF_TREEHEWER_680":{"lore":"Olaf Tretelgja ('Tree-Feller'/'Wood-Cutter') fled Sweden after his father Ingjald's fall and cleared vast forests in Varmland to settle his followers, taking his byname from this felling. When famine struck, the Swedes \u2014 believing he had neglected the sacrifices to Odin \u2014 burned him in his house as an offering, 'giving him to Odin' for good harvests, like his ancestor Domaldi.","fact":"Legendary Yngling figure from Heimskringla, undocumented in contemporary record. This card duplicates the Olaf Tratalja card elsewhere in the shard. The saga firmly makes him son of Ingjald the Swede, NOT (as this bio says) son of a separate 'Ingjald Ill-Ruler King of Sweden' distinct from the other card \u2014 the pedigree has split one saga person into two."},"I350063587798":{"lore":"Olaf Tratalja ('Tree-Feller'), exiled son of Ingjald the Wicked, hewed down the forests of Varmland to clear farmland for the Swedes who followed him into exile. When crop-failure and famine came, his people blamed his lax sacrificing and burned him alive in his hall as a sacrifice to Odin to restore the harvest \u2014 the same grim fate the Ynglings dealt earlier kings.","fact":"A legendary king known only from Ynglingatal and Snorri, with no documentary basis; he is the bridge-figure carrying the Yngling line from Sweden into the Norwegian Vestfold petty-kingdoms. Dates and the Romerike death are genealogical reconstruction."},"I350063587797":{"lore":"Solveig (here garbled 'Solveigh Solva Solisles') appears in the Norwegian genealogies as a wife in the Vestfold line, mother of Halfdan Hvitbeinn ('Whiteleg'). She carries no standalone saga episode; the mangled middle names ('Solva Solisles') are transcription corruption of a single name, Solveig.","fact":"A legendary/genealogical name, not a documented historical person; her role is purely to link Olaf Tretelgja to Halfdan Hvitbeinn. The garbled label and the impossibly compressed dates (born 684, child and death both 704) mark her as a reconstructed pedigree slot rather than a recorded individual."},"X_LORRAINE_GISELA_700":{"lore":"A connective Frankish-aristocracy ancestor, presented as wife of Charibert of Laon and mother of Bertrada of Laon ('Bertha Broadfoot'), who married Pepin the Short and bore Charlemagne. She has no narrative episode of her own \u2014 she is the maternal grandmother-link to Charlemagne.","fact":"Bertrada of Laon is historical (d.783); her parents are debated, with Charibert of Laon usually given as father but her mother only conjecturally named. 'Gisela of Lorraine' is one such conjectural reconstruction, not a securely documented person."},"X_GALICIA_EUDON_700":{"lore":"This figure conflates with Odo (Eudo) the Great, Duke of Aquitaine, who in 721 crushed an Umayyad army at the Battle of Toulouse and whose daughter Lampegia/Munia was married to the Berber lord Munuza. As 'Count of Galicia' he is a garbled, southward-shifted version of that famous Aquitanian duke.","fact":"If meant as Odo of Aquitaine, there is a real historical kernel (a documented early-8th-century duke). But 'Count of Galicia' with daughter Munia is a confused reconstruction blending Aquitanian and Iberian traditions; as labeled here he is largely legendary/erroneous."},"I350063587793":{"lore":"Halfdan Hvitbeinn ('Whiteleg') is in Heimskringla the first of the line to rule in Norway proper: son of Olaf Tretelgja, he became a powerful king over Raumarike, Vestfold, Hedmark and the Uplands, and unlike most Ynglings died peacefully of old age and was buried in a mound at Skiringssal.","fact":"Legendary Norwegian king from the Ynglinga genealogy, undocumented in contemporary record. The death 'in Somme, Picardie' (France) is a pure geocoding artifact \u2014 Snorri has him die and be mound-buried in Vestfold, Norway. Dates are reconstructed."},"X_NORWAY_HALFDAN_HVITBEINN_710":{"lore":"Halfdan Hvitbeinn ('Whiteleg'), son of Olaf Tretelgja, ruled the Uplands and Vestfold and was the first Yngling to die quietly in bed rather than by sacrifice, sword or fire \u2014 a notable break from his doom-laden ancestors. He was buried in a great mound at Skiringssal.","fact":"A legendary king from Heimskringla with no documentary basis; this card duplicates the other Halfdan Hvitbeinn entry in this shard. The bio's '83 years old at the Lindisfarne raid of 793' is a forced anachronism \u2014 the saga places him generations before the Viking Age proper."},"I350063587792":{"lore":"Asa of Hedmark appears in Heimskringla as a wife in the Vestfold line. The most famous saga 'Asa' is the later Asa Haraldsdottir of Agder (the likely Oseberg-ship queen), who had her husband killed to avenge her father \u2014 but this earlier Asa carries no such episode; she is a connective queen-figure.","fact":"A legendary/genealogical name in the Ynglinga pedigree, not independently documented. Her role is purely to link Halfdan Hvitbeinn to the next generation; the dates are reconstructed and the bio's claim that her son was 'Sveide the Sea King' contradicts other cards in the same pedigree."},"X_CARCASSONNE_BULGAR_725":{"lore":"Connective Septimanian/Frankish ancestor, given as father of Bello (Bell\u00f3), the semi-legendary founder of the counts of Carcassonne under Charlemagne. He has no narrative episode; he is a genealogical root for the Carcassonne comital line.","fact":"The counts of Carcassonne begin to enter record only with Bello c.800; earlier 'Bulgar/Bera' ancestors are reconstructed and poorly documented. As labeled, this is a quasi-legendary pedigree link rather than a securely attested historical count."},"X_LEINSTER_CRUINNMAEL_735":{"lore":"A king-list figure of Leinster (Laigin) in early medieval Ireland, presented as son of Ronan and father of Cairpre. The Leinster kingship rotated among rival dynastic septs (Ui Dunlainge, Ui Cheinnselaig) recorded in the Irish annals; he is one such link.","fact":"Early Leinster kings are partly historical, named in annals and king-lists, though genealogies were later groomed for prestige. The specific 'Cruinnmael mac Ronan' with these dates is a reconstructed pedigree entry; the Irish material is more record-grounded than the Norse saga-kings but still not firmly documented for this individual."},"X_ROMERIKE_EYSTEIN_GLUMRA_740":{"lore":"Eystein Glumra ('the Noisy/Clatterer') is the Norwegian jarl remembered as father of Rognvald Eysteinsson, Jarl of More \u2014 the great chieftain who helped Harald Fairhair unify Norway and whose sons founded the earldom of Orkney and (via Rollo) Normandy. Eystein's fame is entirely as the root of that More/Orkney dynasty.","fact":"A quasi-legendary figure standing just at the edge of the historical Viking Age; his son Rognvald of More is semi-historical (late 9th c.). This card's dates (b.740) are far too early and duplicate/conflate with the later Eystein Glumra IV card in this shard; the bio's '53 at Lindisfarne 793' is forced."},"X_PAMPLONA_INIGO_GARCES_750":{"lore":"A connective ancestor of the Pamplona/Navarrese royal line, given as father of Garcia Jimenez. The dynasty's real semi-legendary founder is Inigo Arista (Eneko Aritza), first king of Pamplona c.824, around whom these earlier 'Inigo/Garcia' names cluster.","fact":"The early Pamplona pedigree (Inigo Arista and kin) is semi-legendary, reconstructed from later Navarrese and Arabic sources. 'Inigo Garces of Pamplona' at b.750 is a genealogists' back-projection, not a documented person; the name-order also looks scrambled (Garces is a patronymic of Garcia)."},"I350063587787":{"lore":"Hilf Dagsdottir appears as a wife in the Norwegian petty-king genealogy, given as mother of Gudrod, Ivar and (per the bio) Eystein. She has no individual saga episode; she is a connective queen-figure whose name and exact kin vary between pedigree versions.","fact":"A legendary/genealogical name, undocumented in contemporary record. The chronology is internally broken (a child 'Eystein' dated 800, ten years after her stated 790 death), the hallmark of a patched-together pedigree slot rather than a recorded individual."},"X_BYZANTINE_SABATIUS_755":{"lore":"Connective Byzantine ancestor: 'Sabbatios' is the name tradition gives to the father (or near-ancestor) of Basil I, founder of the Macedonian dynasty. Basil rose from peasant origins through the court to murder Michael III in 867 and seize the throne; this figure is his obscure forebear.","fact":"Basil I is fully historical (r.867-886), but his Macedonian peasant ancestry, including a 'Sabbatios,' is semi-legendary and was later embellished with grand Arsacid/Armenian descent to dignify the dynasty. As labeled here, a poorly documented pedigree root, not a verified individual."},"I350063587781":{"lore":"Thyra of Trondheim appears in the Norwegian genealogy as wife of Ivar, Earl of the Uplands. She carries no standalone saga episode and is a connective queen-figure linking the Upland jarls to the next generation (Eystein).","fact":"A legendary/genealogical name, undocumented in contemporary sources. The chronology is impossible (a son 'Eystein' dated 810, ten years after her stated 800 death), marking her as a reconstructed pedigree slot rather than a recorded historical woman."},"X_OPPLAND_ROGNVALD_770":{"lore":"A king/jarl of Oppland in the Norwegian Upland genealogies, given as son of Eystein Glumra and father of Ascrida. He is a connective ancestor in the More/Orkney-adjacent line, without a distinct saga episode of his own.","fact":"A quasi-legendary figure at the threshold of the Viking Age, undocumented in contemporary record; his existence rests on late genealogical saga material. The bio's '23 at Lindisfarne 793' is a forced anachronism and the dates are reconstructed."},"X_SAXONY_BILLUNG_780":{"lore":"Connective Saxon ancestor evoking the Billung dynasty \u2014 the powerful Saxon noble house (later dukes of Saxony, 10th-11th c.). He is given as father of Oda; the famous early Billung-linked Oda married Liudolf, ancestor of the Ottonian kings. He carries no narrative episode here.","fact":"The Billungs are historical from the 9th-10th centuries, but a 'Billung Count of Saxony' at b.780 is an early, poorly documented back-projection of the dynasty's name. As labeled, a quasi-legendary pedigree root rather than a securely attested individual."},"I350063587782":{"lore":"Ivar, Earl (Jarl) of the Uplands (Oplaendinge), appears in the Norwegian genealogies as a son of Halfdan and ancestor of the Upland/More jarls. He is a connective chieftain-figure of the petty-kingdom era, without a single defining saga episode.","fact":"A legendary/quasi-legendary jarl from late genealogical saga material, undocumented in contemporary record. The dates are reconstructed and the '793 Lindisfarne' anchor is the bio-generator's habitual anachronism, not saga content."},"X_PAMPLONA_GARCIA_JIM_785":{"lore":"Garcia Jimenez, semi-legendary early ruler/founder figure of the Pamplona (Navarre) line, ancestor of the Jimena dynasty that produced Sancho the Great. He is remembered as a root of Navarrese royalty rather than for any single recorded deed.","fact":"The early Garcia/Jimeno names of Pamplona are semi-legendary, reconstructed from later Navarrese and Arabic accounts of the 9th century. 'Garcia Jimenez, Founder of Pamplona' at b.785 is a genealogists' construct; the real documented dynasty begins a generation or more later."},"X_RURIK_ULFR_790":{"lore":"'Ulfr the Fearless' is offered as the Danish-Jutland father of Rurik, legendary founder of the Rus' dynasty. Rurik, per the Primary Chronicle, was the Varangian invited (with Sineus and Truvor) to rule at Novgorod c.862, founding the line that ruled Russia for seven centuries.","fact":"Rurik himself is semi-legendary (possibly identifiable with Rorik of Dorestad); his named father 'Ulfr the Fearless' is not attested in any reliable source and appears to be a later genealogical invention. As labeled, a fabricated/legendary pedigree root."},"X_FRISIA_WALGER_790":{"lore":"A connective Frisian ancestor, given as forebear of Gerulf, the line of the counts of Frisia/Holland. He has no narrative episode; he is a genealogical root for the West-Frisian comital house.","fact":"The counts of Frisia/Holland enter record with Gerulf in the later 9th century; earlier 'Walger' ancestors are reconstructed and undocumented. As labeled, a quasi-legendary pedigree link rather than an attested historical person."},"X_ARPAD_UGEK_790":{"lore":"Ugek (Ugyek) is named in the medieval Hungarian chronicle Gesta Hungarorum as father of Almos and grandfather of Arpad, founder of the Magyar ruling house. By legend Almos's mother Emese dreamed a turul-hawk sired her line (the 'Emese's Dream' myth) before the Magyars rode west into the Carpathian Basin.","fact":"Almos and Arpad are at the edge of history (late 9th c., Magyar conquest c.895); Ugek, their claimed forefather, is a legendary name from a chronicle written ~300 years later, with no contemporary documentation. A traditional pedigree root, not a verified individual."},"I350063587774":{"lore":"Ascrida (Ragnhild) Rognvaldsdottir appears as wife of Eystein Glumra and mother of Rognvald Eysteinsson, Jarl of More \u2014 the chieftain whose sons took Orkney and (as Rollo) Normandy. Her fame is as matriarch of the More/Orkney/Normandy dynasty rather than for a personal episode.","fact":"A quasi-legendary figure at the dawn of the recorded Viking Age; her son Rognvald of More is semi-historical (late 9th c.). Her own dates (b.804, d.890) and name are reconstructed from genealogical saga tradition, not documented record."},"I350063587775":{"lore":"Eystein Glumra ('the Noisy/Clatterer'), Jarl in More/Romerike, is best remembered as father of Rognvald Eysteinsson of More \u2014 the ally of Harald Fairhair whose descendants founded the Orkney earldom and the Duchy of Normandy. His byname marks a loud, blustering manner in the saga tradition.","fact":"A semi-legendary jarl standing at the threshold of history; his son Rognvald is the first securely semi-historical figure of the line (late 9th c.). This entry duplicates/conflates with the earlier 'Eystein Glumra Jarl of Romerike' card; his own dates are reconstructed."},"X_BYZANTINE_INGER_810":{"lore":"Inger (Ingerius) was a Varangian noble at the Byzantine court, remembered as father of Eudokia Ingerina \u2014 mistress of Emperor Michael III and later wife of Basil I, mother of Leo VI. Eudokia's love-triangle at court is one of the most scandalous episodes of 9th-century Byzantium.","fact":"Eudokia Ingerina is fully historical (d.882); her father is identified as a Varangian named Inger, plausible but thinly documented. As an individual 'Inger' rests largely on this filiation; the Norse-at-Constantinople kernel is genuine, the personal details sparse."},"X_ASTURIAS_GUTIERR_810":{"lore":"Gutier (Gutierre) is a name attached to the early counts of the Asturian-Leonese frontier, ancestors of the great Beni G\u00f3mez / Osorio comital house that held Astorga and Salda\u00f1a as Christian Asturias pushed south against al-Andalus during the early Reconquista.","fact":"A shadowy 9th-century Asturian noble known only from much later Leonese genealogies; essentially legendary as drawn here. The 'maternal McCollum bloodline' is a modern family-tree fabrication grafted on; no medieval source connects an Asturian count to any such line."},"X_BAYEUX_MALAHULC_815":{"lore":"Malahulc (Mala Hulk) appears in Dudo of Saint-Quentin's history as a kinsman \u2014 variously brother or uncle \u2014 of Rollo, the Viking founder of Normandy, part of the Norse warband that settled the lower Seine.","fact":"A figure from Dudo's semi-legendary Norman origin-myth (c. 1015), not independently documented; the exact relationships in Dudo are unreliable. The Bayeux birthplace and 815 date are genealogists' guesswork, not record."},"X_ANJOU_TORTULF_815":{"lore":"Tortulf the Forester ('le Forestier') is the legendary woodland-warrior progenitor of the Counts of Anjou in the Gesta Consulum Andegavorum: a man of the forests of Rennes who rose by his prowess, father of Ingelger, first count of Anjou and root of the Angevin (later Plantagenet) line.","fact":"A legendary founder-figure from the 12th-century Angevin house-chronicle, invented to give the counts a heroic origin; no contemporary evidence he existed. The line to Ingelger is itself partly mythologized."},"X_LARRAUN_SANCHO_GARC_820":{"lore":"Set in the cradle of the Kingdom of Pamplona/Navarre, this figure belongs to the \u00cd\u00f1iguez/Jim\u00e9nez dynastic milieu that asserted Basque Christian power against both Franks and the C\u00f3rdoban emirate in the 9th century.","fact":"A poorly attested name from early Navarrese pedigree-building; the specific filiation to Garc\u00eda \u00cd\u00f1iguez and the 'Aznar' son are reconstructions, not solid history. The McCollum-bloodline tie is a modern invention."},"I350063587768":{"lore":"Rollo (Hr\u00f3lfr, 'Ganger-Hr\u00f3lf' the Walker, too big for any horse) was the Viking leader who besieged the Seine and in 911 was granted Normandy by Charles the Simple at Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, becoming its first ruler and great-great-great-grandfather of William the Conqueror.","fact":"A genuinely historical Viking warlord (d. c. 928\u2013932), though his saga ancestry (son of Jarl Rognvald of M\u00f8re) is contested between Norwegian and Danish origins. The birthplace 'Maer, Norway' is saga tradition; he did NOT die in any modern geocoded locale \u2014 he died at Rouen."},"I350063587771":{"lore":"This card conflates Rollo's mother with his daughter: the label 'Hilda Ragnhild Hrolfsdatter' mixes names, and the listed spouse Rognvald the Wise of M\u00f8re was in fact Rollo's FATHER, making this a garbled re-listing of Rollo's mother (Hild/Ragnhild, Rognvald's wife).","fact":"Not a clean distinct person: a transcription mash-up of Rollo's mother and patronymics. The M\u00f8re jarls are saga figures of doubtful historicity; the Orkney birthplace and exact dates are inventions.","phantom":true},"X_SWEDEN_BJORN_HAUGE_855":{"lore":"Bj\u00f6rn at Haugi ('Bj\u00f6rn of the Mound/Barrow') is named in Hervarar saga and Icelandic tradition as a king at Uppsala/Birka, sometimes the Bj\u00f6rn whom Ansgar's mission met, dwelling by the great burial mounds for which he is nicknamed.","fact":"A semi-legendary Swedish king of the Uppsala/Yngling milieu, known only from late sagas and king-lists, not contemporary record; a real 9th-century Birka may underlie the name. The bio's '74 years old in 930' framing is invented chronology."},"X_SWEDEN_ERIC_ANUND_882":{"lore":"Eric (Eir\u00edkr Anundsson) of Uppsala appears in Heimskringla as a powerful Swedish king said to have raided and claimed overlordship in the Baltic and even briefly over parts of Norway, a contemporary-rival figure in Harald Fairhair's saga.","fact":"A saga-king of Uppsala recorded only in Snorri's 13th-century Heimskringla, quasi-legendary with no independent documentation; the Uppland birthplace and precise dates are later constructs."},"X_DONE_AETHELMUND_905":{"lore":"This is a constructed founder for the de Done (Done of Utkinton) family of Cheshire, an Anglo-Saxon/early-medieval thegn placed in the era of Athelstan and the 937 Battle of Brunanburh.","fact":"No record of a 10th-century 'Aethelmund de Done' exists; the de Done family is documented only from after the Norman Conquest. This is a back-projected fabrication, and the 'Maxfield bloodline' link is a modern genealogy graft."},"X_MAINE_HERBERT_WAKE_910":{"lore":"This card grafts the later Wake surname onto an early Count of Maine. Maine was a real frontier county between Normandy, Anjou and Blois, fought over by all three; its early counts (the Hugonids) are historical but murky.","fact":"'Herbert I Wake, Count of Maine' is an anachronistic hybrid \u2014 the Wake family is a much later Anglo-Norman house, and no Count of Maine bore that name in 910. The bio invents events ('Odo of Metz revolt'). Largely fabricated.","phantom":true},"X_YORK_GUNNAR_915":{"lore":"Set in Anglo-Scandinavian Northumbria, this figure is cast as forefather of Thored, the genuine 10th-century ealdorman/earl of York whose daughter \u00c6lfgifu married King \u00c6thelred the Unready.","fact":"Thored of Northumbria is historical, but a father 'Gunnar, Earl of Northumbria' c.915 is not documented \u2014 an invented bridging ancestor. The McCollum-bloodline tie is a modern fabrication."},"X_SWEDEN_BJORN_920":{"lore":"Bj\u00f6rn is named in Heimskringla and Adam of Bremen as a Swedish king and father of Erik the Victorious, placing him at the head of the historical Uppsala dynasty that produced Olof Sk\u00f6tkonung, Sweden's first Christian king.","fact":"A late-saga/early-chronicle Swedish king, quasi-legendary; his existence rests on 11th\u201313th-century sources, though the dynasty he heads (Erik the Victorious onward) is the first solidly historical Swedish royal line. The saga claim of descent from Odin is pure myth."},"X_DONE_AETHELRIC_935":{"lore":"A second constructed de Done thegn, son of the invented Aethelmund, placed amid the West Saxon consolidation under Athelstan and Eadred and the Danish wars in Cheshire.","fact":"Like his 'father', an unrecorded back-projection; the de Done family of Cheshire is post-Conquest in the real record. The Maxfield-bloodline maternal link is modern invention."},"X_DUNKELD_DUNGAL_945":{"lore":"Dungal is cast as an early lay-abbot/lord of Dunkeld, the great Columban church centre of Scotland, and father of Crinan \u2014 the historical lay-abbot of Dunkeld whose son Duncan I became king and whose line founded the royal House of Dunkeld.","fact":"Cr\u00edn\u00e1n of Dunkeld is historical (d. 1045), but his father 'Dungal' is essentially legendary, named only in late and unreliable genealogies. The clan-rivalry detail is generic color, not evidence."},"X_WAKE_WACE_1015":{"lore":"This is a constructed Norman origin for the Wake family, tying the surname to a 'de Vac/Vace' lord in Calvados in the generation of William the Conqueror and the 1066 Halley's-Comet/Hastings year.","fact":"The Wake family is genuinely Anglo-Norman but documented from the late 11th\u201312th century; this 1015 'Wace de Vac' founder is a genealogist's back-projection, not a recorded person."},"X_WAKE_ROBERT_1048":{"lore":"Son of the constructed Wace, placed as a Norman lord living through the Conquest, with the obligatory 1066 comet and Hastings framing.","fact":"No contemporary record of this individual; an invented bridging generation in a back-projected Wake pedigree. The Thury-lineage attribution is a modern family-tree label."},"X_AVESNES_WEDRIC_1055":{"lore":"The Avesnes were a real and powerful lordly house of Hainaut. Wedric is offered as an early progenitor, set against the 1066 comet and the high-feudal Low Countries.","fact":"The historical Avesnes line begins clearly with Wedric/W\u00e9dric 'the Bearded' (W\u00e9d\u00e9ric le Sor) in the later 11th century, so a kernel exists, but this card's exact dates and the McCollum-bloodline maternal tie are reconstructions/inventions."},"X_ORSINI_ODDONE_1070":{"lore":"The Orsini were one of Rome's mightiest baronial clans, producing popes (including Celestine III and Nicholas III) and senators; this card sets an Oddone amid the Investiture Contest \u2014 Canossa 1077, the Norman conquest of Sicily.","fact":"The Orsini are a real Roman dynasty, but their documented continuous line and the name Orsini itself emerge in the 12th\u201313th centuries; an 1070 'Oddone Orsini' is a legendary/back-projected progenitor, not a securely recorded man."},"X_ROGERS_WILLIAM_1085":{"lore":"A constructed founder for a Rogers family of Somerset, placed at the completion of the Domesday Book (1086) and the White Ship disaster (1120).","fact":"No record supports a named Norman 'William Rogers' of Somerset in 1085; surnames like Rogers stabilize much later. An invented bridging ancestor with a modern Thury-lineage label."},"X_AVESNES_WALTER_I_1090":{"lore":"Walter (Wautier) is offered as son of Wedric in the Avesnes line of Hainaut, father of Nicolas de Cond\u00e9 d'Avesnes, amid the Crusading fervor of the early 12th century.","fact":"The Avesnes-Cond\u00e9 connection is historical in outline, but this card's bio is unreliable \u2014 it cites Magna Carta (1215) and the Fourth Crusade as events in the life of a man born 1090, an impossible anachronism. Dates here are not to be trusted."},"MXI132206464074":{"lore":"William FitzWaleran, lord of Sutton Waldron (Dorset) and Steeple Langford, was a minor Anglo-Norman tenant of the post-Conquest settlement, son of Waleran 'the Huntsman', holding his lands through the turmoil of Henry I's reign and the loss of the White Ship heir.","fact":"Plausibly a real minor Anglo-Norman landholder of the early 12th century, though detail is thin and the 'legendary' tier overstates him. The McCollum-bloodline maternal claim is a modern graft."},"X_STOURTON_HENRY_1090":{"lore":"A constructed founder of the Stourton family of Wiltshire (later Barons Stourton), placed amid the death of William Rufus (1100), the White Ship (1120), and the civil-war Anarchy under Stephen and Matilda.","fact":"The Stourton family takes its name from the Wiltshire manor and is documented later; a named 'Henry de Stourton' in 1090 is back-projection. Note the bio even garbles the birthplace as 'St. Ourton'. Thury-lineage tie is modern."},"X_DANYERS_WILLIAM_1095":{"lore":"The Danyers (d'Anyers) were a genuine Cheshire knightly family; this card sets an early William as a post-Conquest Norman lord administering estates in the strategically vital Welsh-border county.","fact":"A back-projected founder \u2014 the Danyers are reliably documented only from the early 13th century (see the 1195 card). This 1095 version is duplicative invention; the Maxfield-bloodline tie is modern.","phantom":true},"X_BOURCHIER_ROBERT_1095":{"lore":"A constructed Norman/Angevin founder for the Bourchier (de Bourcier) family, set against Henry II's reign and the 1170 murder of Thomas Becket.","fact":"The Bourchier family rises to prominence in 13th\u201314th-century England; an 1095 Norman 'Robert de Bourcier' is back-projection. The bio is internally inconsistent (a man born 1095 'in his mid-forties around 1150'). McCollum tie is modern."},"X_DANYERS_WILLIAM_1195":{"lore":"The earliest reliably documented Danyers of Cheshire \u2014 a tenant-in-chief under the Earls of Chester in the early 13th century, father of Thomas de Danyers and root of the family's later distinction at Cr\u00e9cy.","fact":"A genuinely documented early-13th-century Cheshire landholder; the soundest figure of the Danyers cards. The 'legendary' tier is generous but the person is real."},"MXI132206462354":{"lore":"Sir Lawrence St Martin of Littlecote Park, Wiltshire, was an English knight of Edward I's reign, living through the Welsh conquest (1284), the 1290 expulsion of the Jews, and the Scottish wars sparked by Wallace at Stirling Bridge; father of Joanna St Martin.","fact":"A plausibly real minor knightly figure of the St Martin family of Wiltshire/Hampshire, though detail is sparse; the 'legendary' tier overstates a documented late-13th-century gentry knight."},"X_NORBURY_ROBERT_1275":{"lore":"A Cheshire knight of the Norbury family, set as father of John and ancestor of the Maxfield line through Joan, in the era of Edward I.","fact":"A thinly attested late-13th-century Cheshire name used as a genealogical bridge; possibly real but essentially undocumented beyond pedigree assertion."},"X_ROGERS_RICHARD_1275":{"lore":"A Somerset yeoman, cast as great-grandfather of a Thomas Rogers, a humble link in a long Rogers descent.","fact":"A bridging ancestor with no independent documentation \u2014 a name in a constructed pedigree, not a recorded individual. The Thury endpoint is a modern family-tree label."},"X_HOOKE_JOHN_1275":{"lore":"A Devon man cast as father of Robert Hooke and grandfather of Joan Hooke Vyell, a link in the Garrison line.","fact":"An undocumented bridging ancestor in a constructed Devon pedigree; no medieval record substantiates this specific person or dates."},"X_ANDERSON_JOHN_1370":{"lore":"A constructed Scottish 'lord' of the Anderson name, placed under Robert II amid the Wolf of Badenoch's 1390 burning of Elgin Cathedral and the 1411 Battle of Harlaw.","fact":"Anderson ('son of Andrew') is a common surname, not a medieval lordly house; a 14th-century titled 'Scottish lord John Anderson' is fictional pedigree-building. The Davis-bloodline tie is modern."},"X_ANDERSON_THOMAS_1430":{"lore":"Son of the constructed John Anderson, set amid the 1437 assassination of James I, the 1440 'Black Dinner' execution of the Douglas earls, and Gutenberg's press.","fact":"Like his 'father', an invented noble Anderson; no such 15th-century Scottish lord is documented. The vivid historical events are generic backdrop, not biography."},"MXI132210796381":{"lore":"John Hafield (Hatfield) of Barnstaple, Devon, is cast as a late-medieval West-Country man whose long life spanned the Wars of the Roses and the start of Henry VIII's Reformation.","fact":"Possibly a real late-15th/early-16th-century Devon man, but undistinguished; the card's own note admits the 'ancient royal/legendary line' claim is unverified \u2014 that part is fabrication grafted onto an ordinary ancestor."},"MXI132211417549":{"lore":"Ralph Done (mis-spelled 'Doane') of the Done family of Utkinton, Cheshire \u2014 son of John Done \u2014 married Ellena Wilbraham and continued the Cheshire gentry line into the Tudor era.","fact":"A plausibly real Cheshire gentry figure of the Done of Utkinton family. Note the birthplace 'in 1658571, Cheshire' is a raw database-ID geocoding artifact, not a place; and the spouse-death in 1479 (before the 1500 child) is a data error."}};
